TechTales AI Daily — End-to-End Architecture

A reference document explaining how the automated AI news pipeline works, from the original idea through every tool and file that makes it run. Written 15 Aug 2026.

1. The idea

The goal was a fully automated, trustworthy daily AI news briefing, published as an Instagram carousel (and short video) every morning with zero manual writing — but with a human still in control of what actually goes live. Three requirements shaped everything that follows:

Verified, not vibes. No single blog post should be able to appear just because it sounds exciting. Stories need corroboration across sources, and hype/clickbait language gets rejected outright.
Plain language. Press-release and academic phrasing gets rewritten into something a non-technical reader can understand in one scroll.
A human always approves before anything goes public. No matter how good the automation gets, nothing posts to Instagram (or YouTube, once that's connected) without you tapping "Publish" on Telegram first.

Everything below is the system built to satisfy those three requirements, running once a day without you touching a keyboard — until the moment it needs your thumb.

2. The one-sentence version

Every morning, a GitHub Actions robot wakes up, reads ~24 AI news feeds, throws out anything unverified or hype-y, picks the 3 best stories, finds real photos for them, writes plain-English copy, builds a 6-slide Instagram carousel and a 30–58 second vertical video, sends you the whole thing on Telegram, waits (up to 2 hours) for you to tap Publish or Discard, and — only if you tap Publish — posts it live to Instagram.

